From: AgentK <dmitryko@essencesecurity.com>
Subject: [PATCH] uqmi: actually transmit WDS Set Autoconnect Settings

cmd_wds_set_autoconnect_settings_prepare() built the request and then returned
QMI_CMD_DONE, so uqmi exited successfully without ever putting the message on
the wire: --set-autoconnect was silently a no-op. Return QMI_CMD_REQUEST so the
request is sent and its response is checked.

Also zero the request struct. It carries two field pairs, status and roaming:

    struct qmi_wds_set_autoconnect_settings_request {
            struct { unsigned int status : 1; unsigned int roaming : 1; } set;
            struct { uint8_t status;          uint8_t roaming;          } data;
    };

Only status was ever assigned, leaving set.roaming and data.roaming as
uninitialised stack memory. That was harmless while the function returned
QMI_CMD_DONE and nothing was transmitted, but once the message really goes out
a stray set.roaming makes uqmi append a roaming-policy TLV carrying a garbage
value -- nondeterministically, on the one message that decides whether the
modem or the host owns the packet data session.

Signed-off-by: AgentK <dmitryko@essencesecurity.com>
---
--- a/uqmi/commands-wds.c
+++ b/uqmi/commands-wds.c
@@ -406,7 +406,7 @@
 static enum qmi_cmd_result
 cmd_wds_set_autoconnect_settings_prepare(struct qmi_dev *qmi, struct qmi_request *req, struct qmi_msg *msg, char *arg)
 {
-	struct qmi_wds_set_autoconnect_settings_request ac_req;
+	struct qmi_wds_set_autoconnect_settings_request ac_req = {};
 	const char *modes[] = {
 		[QMI_WDS_AUTOCONNECT_SETTING_DISABLED] = "disabled",
 		[QMI_WDS_AUTOCONNECT_SETTING_ENABLED] = "enabled",
@@ -420,7 +420,7 @@
 
 		qmi_set(&ac_req, status, i);
 		qmi_set_wds_set_autoconnect_settings_request(msg, &ac_req);
-		return QMI_CMD_DONE;
+		return QMI_CMD_REQUEST;
 	}
 
 	uqmi_add_error("Invalid value (valid: disabled, enabled, paused)");
